[38;5;10m--depth 1[0m creates a "shallow clone" — it only downloads the latest commit on the default branch, not the full history.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
A normal [38;5;10mgit clone[0m pulls every commit ever made, which for a project like whisper.cpp could be thousands of commits and a much larger download. With [38;5;10m--depth 1[0m, you get just the current state of the files and a single commit, so it's faster and uses less disk space.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The tradeoff is you can't browse history or check out older commits from a shallow clone, but that doesn't matter here since we just need the source code to compile.[0m[0m
